    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Mickey Moniak** (75.98) and **Seiya Suzuki** (75.03) anchor the lineup as our most efficient assets. **Mickey Moniak** benefits from a massive 14% platoon boost against Mahle, complemented by a wind-out projection at the park that adds significant ceiling to his power profile. **Seiya Suzuki** is similarly positioned for a big day; despite the 39% rain risk, his 19% SP skill advantage against the lefty Lodolo and a career-best 8% BvP edge make him a must-start regardless of the precipitation concerns. **Brice Turang** (74.84) also merits attention, as the 100% stolen base opportunity against a slow-delivering pitcher aligns perfectly with our organizational mandate to maximize speed, offsetting the minor park penalty he incurs today.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Josh Naylor** (52.95), who was bypassed despite a solid projection. Ultimately, the high efficiency of **Vinnie Pasquantino** (72.92)\u2014boosted by a 16% park factor and 14% platoon edge against Bradish\u2014rendered **Josh Naylor** the odd man out, as we prioritized the specific matchup advantages over raw base power. While **James Wood** (51.74) faces a tough matchup against an elite arm in Schlittler, his elite 0.432 xwOBA remains too valuable to bench, even with the suppressed SP skill projection. \n\nManagers should keep a close watch on **Austin Riley** (54.18) and **Edwin Arroyo** (53.49); both face weather-related volatility with 32% and 39% rain probabilities, respectively. **Austin Riley** is further pressured by a wind-in factor that dampens his power output, while **Edwin Arroyo** is locked in a spot where any further decline in weather or lineup security could dip his floor below our operational 40.0 threshold. We are monitoring these games closely to ensure no last-minute, low-ceiling pivots are required.",
